\title{Tight Length Bounds for Distinguishing Experiments\\ on Deterministic Moore Machines}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{21 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the length of the shortest experiment that separates two behaviourally
inequivalent initial states of deterministic Moore machines over a common input alphabet
$A$ and a common observation type $O$. We give a complete, self-contained development of
three linked results. First, a product-automaton fixpoint argument on the increasing
chain of ``distinguishable within $k$ steps'' relations shows that inequivalent initial
states of machines with state sets $S$ and $T$ are separated by a word of length
$< |S| \cdot |T|$. Second, an abstract chain-stabilisation theorem for decreasing chains
of equivalence relations on a finite set --- any such chain whose bottom level is already
non-trivial stabilises by index $|U| - 2$ --- applied to the disjoint union $S \sqcup T$,
sharpens the bound to the linear estimate $|S| + |T| - 2$, which subsumes the quadratic
one. Third, we prove the linear bound extremal for \emph{every} pair of state-set sizes
by an explicit two-parameter family over the unary alphabet: a saturating tail of $n$
states raced against a cycle of $m$ states, whose first disagreement occurs at time
exactly $n + m - 2$. As consequences we obtain an explicit complete finite test suite ---
all words of length at most $|S| + |T| - 2$, of cardinality at most
$(|A| + 1)^{|S| + |T| - 2}$ --- and decidability of behavioural equivalence via a
computable bounded-agreement recursion. We complement these with a matching negative
result: once the state set may be infinite, every behaviour $A^* \to \B$ is realised by a
machine, and consequently no fixed finite test suite is complete. The resulting dichotomy
identifies finiteness of the state space as exactly the property that converts
decidability into a bounded test plan.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Moore machine, behavioural equivalence, distinguishing
sequence, Moore bound, partition refinement, finite test suite, conformance testing,
Fine--Wilf periodicity.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

A deterministic Moore machine is the simplest useful model of a reactive system: an
internal state, a deterministic reaction to each input symbol, and an observable output
attached to each state. Two initial states are \emph{behaviourally equivalent} if no
experiment --- no finite input word --- produces different observations. Behavioural
equivalence is the semantic notion of ``same system'' for such models: it is exactly the
relation that must hold between a specification and a correct implementation.

The definition quantifies over infinitely many words. This raises the fundamental
question of black-box testing: \emph{if two initial states are not behaviourally
equivalent, how long must a distinguishing experiment be?}

Any bound $B(|S|,|T|)$ answers three questions at once. It makes equivalence decidable
(check all words of length $\le B$). It yields an explicit finite test suite for
conformance testing. And it quantifies the cost of black-box verification in terms of
structural size alone, with no reference to the machines' internal wiring.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Product bound} (Theorem~\ref{thm:product}): inequivalent initial states
    admit a distinguishing word of length $< |S| \cdot |T|$, via a saturating fixpoint
    chain in $S \times T$.
  \item \textbf{Abstract refinement stabilisation} (Theorem~\ref{thm:stab}): a decreasing
    chain of equivalence relations on a finite set $U$, non-trivial at level $0$,
    stabilises at some index $\le |U| - 2$.
  \item \textbf{Moore bound} (Theorem~\ref{thm:moore2}): the previous result applied to
    the disjoint union $S \sqcup T$ gives the linear bound $|S| + |T| - 2$, which
    subsumes the product bound since $n + m - 2 < nm$ for $n,m \ge 1$.
  \item \textbf{Extremality for all sizes} (Theorem~\ref{thm:extremal}): an explicit
    unary two-parameter family attaining $|S| + |T| - 2$ for every pair of sizes; and the
    absence of any bound independent of the state counts.
  \item \textbf{Explicit complete test suite} (Theorem~\ref{thm:suite}) of size at most
    $(|A| + 1)^{|S| + |T| - 2}$, together with decidability of equivalence.
  \item \textbf{The infinite-state barrier and dichotomy}
    (Theorems~\ref{thm:free}, \ref{thm:nofinite}, \ref{thm:dichotomy}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Relation to classical automata theory}

The linear bound is the Moore-machine analogue of the classical fact that inequivalent
states of a deterministic automaton are separated by a word of length below the number of
states, obtained by partition refinement. What we add is (i) a fully abstract formulation
of the refinement engine, decoupled from machines; (ii) a \emph{two-machine} statement
with the sharp constant $|S| + |T| - 2$, obtained by transporting the single-machine
result across the disjoint union; (iii) a general extremal family showing the constant is
exact for every pair of sizes, over a unary alphabet, where the phenomenon becomes a
statement about the interaction of a preperiod and a period; and (iv) the exact
complementary negative result delimiting the theory.

\section{Preliminaries}

Throughout, $A$ is a set of input symbols, $O$ a set of observations, $A^*$ the set of
finite words over $A$, $\varepsilon$ the empty word, $|w|$ the length of $w$, and
$a \cdot w$ the word $w$ prefixed by $a$.

\begin{definition}[Moore machine]
A \emph{deterministic Moore machine} over $(A,O)$ with state set $S$ is a pair
$M = (\delta,\lambda)$ with a total transition function $\delta \colon S \times A \to S$
and an output function $\lambda \colon S \to O$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Run and observation]
$\run_M(s,\varepsilon) = s$ and $\run_M(s, a\cdot w) = \run_M(\delta(s,a), w)$; the
\emph{observation} is $\obs_M(s,w) = \lambda(\run_M(s,w))$.
\end{definition}

Two immediate consequences, used constantly, are
\begin{equation}\label{eq:unfold}
\obs_M(s,\varepsilon) = \lambda(s), \qquad \obs_M(s, a\cdot w) = \obs_M(\delta(s,a), w).
\end{equation}
Equation~\eqref{eq:unfold} is the \emph{coinductive unfolding}: every proof below is at
bottom an exploitation of it.

\begin{definition}[Behavioural equivalence]
Let $M$ have state set $S$ and $N$ have state set $T$, over the same $A$ and $O$. States
$s \in S$ and $t \in T$ are \emph{behaviourally equivalent}, written $s \approx t$, if
$\obs_M(s,w) = \obs_N(t,w)$ for all $w \in A^*$. A word $w$ with
$\obs_M(s,w) \neq \obs_N(t,w)$ is a \emph{distinguishing experiment}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Bounded distinguishability and agreement]
For $k \in \N$,
\[
D_k(s,t) :\iff \exists w,\ |w| \le k \wedge \obs_M(s,w) \neq \obs_N(t,w), \qquad
E_k(s,t) :\iff \forall w,\ |w| \le k \Rightarrow \obs_M(s,w) = \obs_N(t,w).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Duality]\label{lem:duality}
$\neg D_k(s,t) \iff E_k(s,t)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Negating an existential over a conjunction yields a universal over an implication.
\end{proof}

\section{The product bound}

\begin{lemma}[Base level]\label{lem:base}
$D_0(s,t) \iff \lambda_M(s) \neq \lambda_N(t)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A word of length $\le 0$ is $\varepsilon$, and $\obs(s,\varepsilon) = \lambda(s)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[One-step unfolding]\label{lem:step}
For all $k$,
\[
D_{k+1}(s,t) \iff \lambda_M(s) \neq \lambda_N(t) \ \vee\ \exists a \in A,\
D_k(\delta_M(s,a), \delta_N(t,a)).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) A separating $w$ with $|w| \le k+1$ is either $\varepsilon$, so the
outputs differ, or $a \cdot v$ with $|v| \le k$, and by \eqref{eq:unfold} $v$ separates
the $a$-successors. ($\Leftarrow$) Symmetric: use $\varepsilon$ in the first case and
$a\cdot v$ in the second.
\end{proof}

Dualising through Lemma~\ref{lem:duality} gives the computational form
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Erec}
E_0(s,t) \iff \lambda_M(s) = \lambda_N(t), \qquad
E_{k+1}(s,t) \iff \lambda_M(s) = \lambda_N(t) \wedge \forall a,\
E_k(\delta_M(s,a), \delta_N(t,a)).
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity]\label{lem:mono}
If $k \le l$ and $D_k(s,t)$ then $D_l(s,t)$; dually $E_l \Rightarrow E_k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A witness of length $\le k$ has length $\le l$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Constant observation]\label{lem:const}
If $\lambda_M(s') = \lambda_N(t')$ for all $s' \in S$, $t' \in T$, then
$\obs_M(s',w) = \obs_N(t',w)$ for all $w$ and all $s',t'$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $w$ using \eqref{eq:unfold}, generalising over the states.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Saturation]\label{thm:sat}
Suppose $D_{k+1}(s',t') \Rightarrow D_k(s',t')$ for all $s',t'$. Then for every
$j \in \N$ and all $s',t'$, $D_{k+j}(s',t') \Rightarrow D_k(s',t')$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
First, by induction on $j$, the shifted statement
$D_{k+j+1}(s',t') \Rightarrow D_{k+j}(s',t')$ holds for all $s',t'$. The base case is the
hypothesis. For the step, assume it at level $j$ and let $D_{(k+j+1)+1}(s',t')$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:step} either the outputs differ --- then $\varepsilon$ witnesses
$D_{k+j+1}(s',t')$ --- or some $a$ has $D_{k+j+1}$ at the successors, hence $D_{k+j}$
there by the induction hypothesis, and Lemma~\ref{lem:step} again gives
$D_{k+j+1}(s',t')$. The theorem follows by a second induction on $j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Product bound]\label{thm:product}
Let $S,T$ be finite. If $s \in S$ and $t \in T$ are not behaviourally equivalent, then
some $w$ with $|w| < |S| \cdot |T|$ satisfies $\obs_M(s,w) \neq \obs_N(t,w)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Put $n = |S| \cdot |T|$ and $\mathcal{D}_k = \{(x,y) : D_k(x,y)\} \subseteq S \times T$.
By Lemma~\ref{lem:mono} the chain increases and $|\mathcal{D}_k| \le n$.

\emph{Nonempty at level $0$.} If all cross-outputs agreed, Lemma~\ref{lem:const} would
make $s \approx t$; so some pair has differing outputs and $\mathcal{D}_0 \neq \emptyset$
by Lemma~\ref{lem:base}.

\emph{Stabilisation before index $n$.} Otherwise $\mathcal{D}_k \subsetneq
\mathcal{D}_{k+1}$ for all $k < n$, so $|\mathcal{D}_k| \ge k+1$ by induction, and
$k = n$ contradicts $|\mathcal{D}_n| \le n$. Hence some $k < n$ has
$D_{k+1} \Rightarrow D_k$ everywhere.

\emph{Collapse.} Some word $w$ separates $s$ and $t$, so $D_{k+|w|}(s,t)$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:mono}; Theorem~\ref{thm:sat} gives $D_k(s,t)$, i.e.\ a separating word of
length $\le k < n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite test characterisation]\label{thm:testchar}
For finite $S,T$: $s \approx t$ if and only if $\obs_M(s,w) = \obs_N(t,w)$ for all $w$
with $|w| < |S| \cdot |T|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Forward is trivial; backward is Theorem~\ref{thm:product} contrapositively.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:EK}
If $|S| \cdot |T| > 0$ then $s \approx t \iff E_{|S|\cdot|T| - 1}(s,t)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Decidability]\label{thm:dec}
Let $A$ be finite, $O$ have decidable equality, and $S,T$ be finite and nonempty. Then
$s \approx t$ is decidable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$E_k$ is decidable by recursion on $k$ using \eqref{eq:Erec}: the base case is a
decidable equality test, and the successor case is a conjunction of that test with a
finite conjunction over $a \in A$. Corollary~\ref{cor:EK} reduces equivalence to
$E_{|S|\cdot|T|-1}$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Moore bound: from quadratic to linear}

\begin{theorem}[Refinement stabilisation]\label{thm:stab}
Let $U$ be a finite set and $R_0 \supseteq R_1 \supseteq \cdots$ a decreasing chain of
equivalence relations on $U$ with $R_0$ non-trivial (some pair unrelated). Then there is
$k \le |U| - 2$ with $R_k = R_{k+1}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $[x]_k$ for the $R_k$-class of $x$; then $[x]_k = [y]_k \iff R_k(x,y)$. Let
$\Pi_k$ be the level-$k$ partition and $b_k = |\Pi_k|$. Define the saturation operator
$G_k(C) = \{z : \exists y \in C,\ R_k(z,y)\}$. Then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sat}
G_k([x]_{k+1}) = [x]_k \qquad (x \in U),
\end{equation}
since $z$ related to some $y$ with $R_{k+1}(x,y)$ satisfies $R_k(x,y)$ by monotonicity
and hence $R_k(x,z)$; conversely $x \in [x]_{k+1}$. Thus $\Pi_k = G_k(\Pi_{k+1})$ and
$b_k \le b_{k+1}$.

If the chain is unstable at $k$, pick $x,y$ with $R_k(x,y)$ and $\neg R_{k+1}(x,y)$: then
$[x]_{k+1} \neq [y]_{k+1}$ but $G_k([x]_{k+1}) = [x]_k = [y]_k = G_k([y]_{k+1})$ by
\eqref{eq:sat}, so $G_k$ is not injective on $\Pi_{k+1}$ and $b_k < b_{k+1}$.

Non-triviality gives $b_0 \ge 2$, and always $b_k \le |U|$. If the chain were unstable at
every $k \le |U|-2$ then $b_k \ge 2+k$ for all $k \le |U|-1$, and $k = |U|-1$ gives
$b_{|U|-1} \ge |U|+1$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

For a single machine $M$ on $U$, the relations $E_k$ (with $N = M$) are equivalence
relations --- reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity are inherited pointwise from
equality of observations --- and $E_{k+1} \subseteq E_k$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:mono}.

\begin{theorem}[Moore bound, one machine]\label{thm:moore1}
Let $M$ have finite state set $U$ and let $x,y \in U$ be behaviourally inequivalent. Then
some $w$ with $|w| \le |U| - 2$ satisfies $\obs_M(x,w) \neq \obs_M(y,w)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $E_0$ were trivial (all pairs related), all outputs would be equal and
Lemma~\ref{lem:const} would force $x \approx y$. So Theorem~\ref{thm:stab} gives
$k \le |U|-2$ with $E_k = E_{k+1}$, i.e.\ (Lemma~\ref{lem:duality})
$D_{k+1} \Rightarrow D_k$ everywhere. Some $w$ separates $x$ and $y$, so
$D_{k+|w|}(x,y)$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:sat} collapses this to $D_k(x,y)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Sum machine]
$M \oplus N$ has state set $S \sqcup T$, with
$\delta(\inl s, a) = \inl \delta_M(s,a)$, $\delta(\inr t, a) = \inr \delta_N(t,a)$,
$\lambda(\inl s) = \lambda_M(s)$ and $\lambda(\inr t) = \lambda_N(t)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Embedding]\label{lem:embed}
$\obs_{M \oplus N}(\inl s, w) = \obs_M(s,w)$ and
$\obs_{M \oplus N}(\inr t, w) = \obs_N(t,w)$ for all $w$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $w$, generalising over the state; the transition commutes with the
injections by definition.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Moore bound, two machines]\label{thm:moore2}
Let $S,T$ be finite and $s \in S$, $t \in T$ behaviourally inequivalent. Then some $w$
with $|w| \le |S| + |T| - 2$ satisfies $\obs_M(s,w) \neq \obs_N(t,w)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:embed}, $\inl s$ and $\inr t$ are inequivalent states of $M \oplus N$,
which has $|S| + |T|$ states; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:moore1} and transport the separating
word back.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Subsumption]\label{prop:subsume}
For all integers $n,m \ge 1$, $n + m - 2 < nm$. Hence Theorem~\ref{thm:moore2} implies
Theorem~\ref{thm:product}.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For $n = 1$ the claim is $m - 1 < m$, symmetrically for $m = 1$. For $n,m \ge 2$,
$(n-1)(m-1) \ge 1$ gives $nm \ge n + m$, so $n+m-2 < n+m \le nm$.
\end{proof}

\section{Extremality}

\subsection{A first family: the saturating counter}

\begin{definition}
For $n \in \N$ the \emph{counter machine} $C_n$ has states $\{0,\dots,n\}$ over the unary
alphabet, $\delta(i) = \min(i+1,n)$ and $\lambda(i) = [\,i = n\,]$. The \emph{sink
machine} has one state and constant output $\mathrm{false}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:counter}
Running $C_n$ from $i$ along a word of length $\ell$ lands in $\min(i+\ell, n)$; hence
$\obs_{C_n}(0,w) = [\,n \le |w|\,]$ while the sink always outputs $\mathrm{false}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $w$, generalising over $i$ and splitting on $i < n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact separation for the counter family]\label{thm:counter}
A word $w$ separates the initial state $0$ of $C_n$ from the sink iff $|w| \ge n$. Hence
the shortest separating word has length exactly $n = |S|\cdot|T| - 1 = |S| + |T| - 2$
with $|S| = n+1$, $|T| = 1$: both bounds are attained for every $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Lemma~\ref{lem:counter}; the arithmetic identities are $(n+1)\cdot 1 - 1 =
n$ and $(n+1)+1-2 = n$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The general two-parameter family}

Write $n = n'+1$, $m = m'+1$ and set $r = (n'+m') \bmod m$.

\begin{definition}[Tail and cycle machines]
Over the unary alphabet: $\Tail_{n',m'}$ has states $\{0,\dots,n'\}$, transition
$\delta(i) = \min(i+1,n')$, and output $\lambda(i) = [\,i < n' \wedge i \bmod m = r\,]$;
$\Cyc_{n',m'}$ has states $\{0,\dots,m'\}$, transition $\delta(j) = (j+1) \bmod m$, and
output $\lambda(j) = [\,j = r\,]$. Both start in state $0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Behaviours]\label{lem:behav}
For $|w| = \ell$,
\[
\obs_{\Tail}(0,w) = [\,\min(\ell,n') < n' \wedge \min(\ell,n') \bmod m = r\,], \qquad
\obs_{\Cyc}(0,w) = [\,\ell \bmod m = r\,].
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The runs, computed by induction on $w$, reach $\min(\ell,n')$ and $\ell \bmod m$
respectively; apply the output functions.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Blocking window]\label{lem:window}
If $n' \le \ell < n'+m'$ then $\ell \bmod m \neq r$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $\ell \equiv r \equiv n'+m' \pmod m$ then $m \mid (n'+m'-\ell)$ while
$0 < n'+m'-\ell \le m' < m$, and a positive multiple of $m$ is at least $m$.
\end{proof}

The $m$ consecutive integers $n', n'+1, \dots, n'+m'$ form a complete residue system
modulo $m$, and by the choice of $r$ the unique one congruent to $r$ is the last.

\begin{theorem}[Extremality for every pair of sizes]\label{thm:extremal}
For all $n',m' \in \N$, the machines $\Tail_{n',m'}$ (with $n = n'+1$ states) and
$\Cyc_{n',m'}$ (with $m = m'+1$ states) have behaviourally inequivalent initial states,
every separating word has length $\ge n+m-2 = n'+m'$, and some word of length exactly
$n+m-2$ separates them. Hence the bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:moore2} is attained for every
pair of sizes, over a unary alphabet.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $\ell = |w| < n'+m'$. If $\ell < n'$ then $\min(\ell,n') = \ell < n'$ and by
Lemma~\ref{lem:behav} the tail outputs $[\,\ell \bmod m = r\,]$, exactly the cycle's
output. If $n' \le \ell$ then $\min(\ell,n') = n'$, the tail's first conjunct fails and it
outputs $\mathrm{false}$, while Lemma~\ref{lem:window} makes the cycle's output
$\mathrm{false}$ as well. At $\ell = n'+m'$ the tail still outputs $\mathrm{false}$ while
the cycle outputs $[\,(n'+m') \bmod m = r\,] = \mathrm{true}$; the states are therefore
inequivalent and separated exactly at that length.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No uniform bound]\label{cor:nouniform}
For every $k \in \N$ there is a pair of finite Moore machines with inequivalent initial
states all of whose separating words have length at least $k$. Hence no length bound
independent of the state counts exists.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Take $C_k$ against the sink and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:counter}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The periodicity reading}

Over a unary alphabet a machine's behaviour is the infinite Boolean sequence
$(\obs(s_0,\bullet^\ell))_{\ell \ge 0}$, and an $m$-state machine produces a sequence
that is eventually periodic with preperiod plus period at most $m$.
Theorem~\ref{thm:extremal} therefore reads: a purely periodic sequence of period $m$ and
a sequence that becomes constant after $n-1$ terms can agree on the first $n+m-2$
positions and must disagree by position $n+m-2$. This is the automata-theoretic shadow of
the classical Fine--Wilf periodicity phenomenon, in which the length of possible agreement
between differently-periodic words is controlled by the periods and their greatest common
divisor.

\section{An explicit finite test suite}

Assume $A$ finite with decidable equality.

\begin{definition}[Bounded-length suite]
$W_0 = \{\varepsilon\}$ and $W_{k+1} = W_k \cup \{a \cdot v : a \in A,\ v \in W_k\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:suite}
$w \in W_k \iff |w| \le k$, and $|W_k| \le (|A|+1)^k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Membership by induction on $k$; cardinality by induction using
$|W_{k+1}| \le |W_k| + |A|\cdot|W_k| = (|A|+1)|W_k|$, the second set being the image of
$A \times W_k$ under $(a,v) \mapsto a\cdot v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Complete finite test suite]\label{thm:suite}
For finite $S,T$ put $\TS(A,S,T) = W_{|S|+|T|-2}$. Then for all machines $M$ on $S$, $N$
on $T$ and all $s,t$,
\[
s \approx t \iff \forall w \in \TS(A,S,T),\ \obs_M(s,w) = \obs_N(t,w),
\]
and $|\TS(A,S,T)| \le (|A|+1)^{|S|+|T|-2}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Forward is trivial. Backward: if $s \not\approx t$, Theorem~\ref{thm:moore2} yields a
separating word of length $\le |S|+|T|-2$, which lies in the suite by
Lemma~\ref{lem:suite}. The cardinality bound is Lemma~\ref{lem:suite}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Every omitted word matters]\label{prop:omit}
Let $W$ be any set of words and $w \notin W$. Then the behaviours $f \equiv
\mathrm{false}$ and $g = [\,\cdot = w\,]$ agree on all of $W$ and differ at $w$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For $v \in W$ we have $v \neq w$, so $g(v) = \mathrm{false} = f(v)$, while
$g(w) = \mathrm{true} \neq f(w)$.
\end{proof}

\section{The infinite-state barrier}

\begin{definition}[Free machine]
For $f \colon A^* \to \B$, the \emph{free machine} $F_f$ has state set $A^*$, transition
$\delta(s,a) = s \cdot a$ and output $\lambda(s) = f(s)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Universality]\label{thm:free}
$\obs_{F_f}(\varepsilon, w) = f(w)$ for all $f$ and $w$. Hence every function
$A^* \to \B$ is the behaviour of an initial state of some Moore machine.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By induction $\run_{F_f}(s,w) = s\cdot w$; with $s = \varepsilon$ this is $w$, and
applying $\lambda$ gives $f(w)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No finite test suite]\label{thm:nofinite}
Let $A$ be nonempty and $W$ a finite set of words. Then there are $f \neq g$ with
$f(w) = g(w)$ for all $w \in W$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $\mu = \max\{|w| : w \in W\}$ (with $\max\emptyset = 0$), pick $a \in A$ and set
$u = a^{\mu+1} \notin W$. Take $f \equiv \mathrm{false}$ and $g = [\,\cdot = u\,]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every finite $W$ there are two machines on the infinite state set $A^*$ whose initial
states agree on every word of $W$ yet are behaviourally inequivalent.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Combine Theorems~\ref{thm:free} and \ref{thm:nofinite}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-test dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Fix a nonempty finite alphabet $A$, an observation type $O$, and $k \in \N$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item for every pair of finite-state machines with $|S| + |T| - 2 \le k$ and all
    $s,t$, agreement on all of $W_k$ is equivalent to $s \approx t$; and
  \item there exist behaviours $f \neq g$ whose free machines agree on the whole of $W_k$
    yet whose initial states are inequivalent.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(1) is Theorem~\ref{thm:suite} together with Lemma~\ref{lem:suite} and monotonicity of
$W_\bullet$; (2) is Proposition~\ref{prop:omit} applied to $a^{k+1} \notin W_k$, realised
as machines by Theorem~\ref{thm:free}.
\end{proof}

Testing an infinite-state black box is not merely expensive: no finite amount of it is
evidence of anything. Testing a finite-state black box of known size is not merely
possible: a bounded, precomputable list of experiments settles the question completely.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm 1 (Bounded agreement, recursive).}
Decide $E_k(s,t)$ by \eqref{eq:Erec}: compare outputs, and for $k > 0$ recurse on all
$a$-successors with budget $k-1$. Naively this explores $O(|A|^k)$ paths --- the direct
evaluation of the test suite --- but needs no space beyond the recursion stack.

\paragraph{Algorithm 2 (Fixpoint refinement on the product).}
Initialise $\mathcal{D} = \{(x,y) : \lambda_M(x) \neq \lambda_N(y)\}$ and repeatedly add
every pair $(x,y)$ having some $a$ with $(\delta_M(x,a),\delta_N(y,a)) \in \mathcal{D}$
until closure. By Theorem~\ref{thm:sat} the result is the full inequivalence relation and
by Theorem~\ref{thm:product} at most $|S|\cdot|T|$ rounds suffice; recording the round at
which each pair enters yields the exact shortest distinguishing length.

\paragraph{Algorithm 3 (Shortest separating word by product BFS).}
Breadth-first search in the product graph from $(s,t)$, following $a$ from $(x,y)$ to
$(\delta_M(x,a),\delta_N(y,a))$, stopping at the first reached pair with differing
outputs and reconstructing the word from the BFS tree; complexity
$O(|A|\cdot|S|\cdot|T|)$. Theorem~\ref{thm:moore2} guarantees the answer never exceeds
$|S|+|T|-2$, an inequality that doubles as a runtime assertion.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Conformance testing.}
For an implementation with at most $|S|$ states claimed to conform to a specification
with $|T|$ states, Theorem~\ref{thm:suite} supplies a finite, precomputed test plan whose
completeness is unconditional; the bound $(|A|+1)^{|S|+|T|-2}$ is the worst case for
blind enumeration, while Algorithm 3 finds the shortest witness in linear time in the
product size when the implementation is a white box.

\paragraph{Sequential equivalence checking.}
Two synchronous circuits with $n$ and $m$ reachable latch configurations, if
distinguishable at all, are distinguished by a stimulus of length at most $n+m-2$: an
explicit and tight unrolling depth for bounded model checking of ``the outputs always
agree''.

\paragraph{Model learning.}
When an upper bound on the target's state count is known, Theorem~\ref{thm:suite}
converts an equivalence oracle into a finite membership-query loop, making active
automata learning terminating with explicit query complexity.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The development has a three-part shape: a coarse but transparent bound
($|S|\cdot|T|$, from the product fixpoint); a sharp bound ($|S|+|T|-2$, from abstract
refinement stabilisation); and matching extremal examples for every pair of sizes over
the smallest possible alphabet. The abstract engine, Theorem~\ref{thm:stab}, is where the
mathematics lives: it mentions no machines, and any monotone refinement process on a
finite carrier inherits its conclusion.

\paragraph{Fine--Wilf calibration of unary experiments.}
For unary machines with $n$ and $m$ states whose reachable parts are a $(p_1,q_1)$- and a
$(p_2,q_2)$-rho (preperiod $p_i$, period $q_i$, $p_i + q_i \le$ size), we conjecture that
the shortest distinguishing word has length at most
\[
\max(p_1,p_2) + q_1 + q_2 - \gcd(q_1,q_2) - 1,
\]
and that this is attained. A unary machine's behaviour \emph{is} an eventually periodic
word, so the distinguishing length is the ``first mismatch'' quantity governed by the
Fine--Wilf periodicity theorem, with the preperiod contributing additively and the
periods through their gcd.

\paragraph{Reachability-relativised bounds.}
We expect $|S|$ and $|T|$ to be replaceable by the numbers $r_S, r_T$ of states reachable
from the initial states, giving $r_S + r_T - 2$, unimprovable for minimal reachable
machines: the refinement chain never sees unreachable states, so one instantiates the
abstract engine with the reachable subtype in place of $S \sqcup T$.

\paragraph{Adaptive versus non-adaptive testing.}
The suite $W_k$ is non-adaptive. We conjecture that adaptivity cannot reduce the
\emph{number} of tests needed to separate all inequivalent pairs of $n$-state machines by
more than a logarithmic factor, so the exponential suite size is intrinsic.

\paragraph{Beyond determinism, and quantitative settings.}
The saturation argument uses determinism only through the fact that the pair $(x,y)$
determines the successors; for nondeterministic or probabilistic models the chain lives
on a lattice of sets or a convex space and block counting must be replaced by a rank or
dimension count. Replacing Boolean observations by semiring values turns equivalence into
equality of formal power series, where Hankel-rank arguments give bounds in terms of rank
rather than state count; unifying the combinatorial and linear-algebraic bounds in a
single statement degenerating to Theorem~\ref{thm:moore2} over the Boolean semiring would
be a satisfying synthesis.

